<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">European Commission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1958</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The European Commission is the executive body of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, which proposes legislation, enforces the treaties and manages common policies. It grew from the High Authority of the European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">Steel</a> Community and the commissions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> and Euratom, which merged in 1967. Its president is one of the most powerful figures in European politics; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourgers">Luxembourgers</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaston-thorn">Gaston Thorn</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacques-santer">Jacques Santer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-claude-juncker">Jean-Claude Juncker</a> each held the post.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/european-commission.jpg" alt="European Commission" width="320" height="178" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EmDee · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belgique_-_Bruxelles_-_Schuman_-_Berlaymont_-_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
